{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-21g-315-fall-2011", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["21G.315"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Cross-Cultural Perspectives on Contemporary French Society", "course_slug": "21g-315-cross-cultural-perspectives-on-contemporary-french-society-fall-2011", "term": "Fall", "year": 2011, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2011", "language": "en", "departments": ["Global Studies and Languages"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Sabine Levet"], "subject_primary": "Language", "subject_secondary": ["French", "Literature", "International Literature", "Social Science", "Anthropology", "Cultural Anthropology"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "Language", "specialties": ["French"]}, {"name": "Literature", "specialties": ["International Literature"]}]}, {"name": "Social Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Anthropology", "specialties": ["Cultural Anthropology"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Written Assignments"], "description": "This course is an intermediate subject designed to help students gradually build an in-depth understanding of France. The course focuses on French attitudes and values regarding education, work, family and institutions, and deals with the differing notions that underlie interpersonal interactions and communication styles, such as politeness, friendship and formality. Using a Web comparative, cross-cultural approach, students explore a variety of French and American materials, then analyze and compare them using questionnaires, opinion polls, news reports (in different media), as well as a variety of historical, anthropological and literary texts. Throughout the course, attention is given to the development of relevant linguistics skills. This course is recommended for students planning to study and work in France an